🎁 Bonus Learning
1. Try a Large Number

Python supports very large integers, so the loop version can calculate factorials such as 20! and 500! without the fixed-size integer overflow that occurs in some other programming languages.

Try entering 20 and 500 to observe how quickly the result grows.

2. Understand the Recursion Limit

Try entering 5000.

The recursive function will likely raise a RecursionError because Python limits the depth of recursive calls. Since your program calls both functions for each valid input, this error can interrupt the program before both results are displayed.

The loop version avoids this recursion-depth limitation and is generally the better choice for calculating large factorials in this exercise.

Bonus status: The two calculation methods, negative-number handling, and repeated-input loop are implemented. The large-number and recursion-limit experiments are optional manual tests, not extra features in the code.


